+# Decision: Change delivery approach
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event; editors submit commands over HTTPS and the server assigns each committed edit one event revision. How that committed revision reaches the up to 800 connected browsers is a separate question from how it is authorized and stored, and it shapes three downstream components at once: the change store (what it must retain and expose), delivery (how it gets changes out), and browser sync (what the browser must do to stay current). Getting it wrong is expensive to unwind, since the change store's revision model, delivery's session model, and the browser's reconnect logic are all built around the choice.
+
+The HLD's freshness goal — committed changes visible within 15 seconds under normal connectivity — is met by every option in the domain; it does not by itself distinguish them. Likewise, the tenet that event data contains only stall IDs and operational statuses, not customer records, removes data sensitivity as a reason to avoid a managed/hosted option; it doesn't favor one option over another either. The real differences are in who bears the ongoing cost of running the mechanism, and in how fast a change actually appears.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Operational burden of connection management** (moderate) — who owns connection capacity, socket scaling, and reconnect edge cases at 800 concurrent browsers with opening-time bursts of 20 edits/second.
+- **Recurring cost and provider coupling** (major) — whether the mechanism carries an ongoing vendor bill and ties the board's core update path to a third party's API, pricing, and availability.
+- **Update latency beyond the 15-second floor** (minor) — all options clear the goal; push-based delivery narrows the gap further and avoids repeated reads, where polling leaves a steady few seconds of staleness and a read per browser per interval.
+
+No existing tenet speaks directly to provider coupling — the closest, "self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted," only confirms neither is barred, not which to prefer. Weighing it as a major criterion here is a judgment call this decision makes on its own; if future delivery-adjacent choices keep weighing it the same way, it is worth promoting to a tenet.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Browsers re-request board state on a five-second timer using cursor-based queries against the change store; delivery stays plain request/response with no persistent connection state.
+
+- Operational burden: **strong fit** — no connection capacity to size, no reconnect logic; a dropped request just retries on the next tick.
+- Recurring cost and provider coupling: **strong fit** — no vendor, no recurring fee, no third-party dependency on the update path.
+- Update latency: **weak fit** — up to five seconds of added staleness on top of processing time, and every browser re-reads on every tick, so read load scales linearly with connected browsers and compounds during opening-time bursts.
+
+Summary: cheapest to run and simplest to reason about, paid for in steady lag and read load that grows with browser count.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+The server holds a persistent WebSocket connection per browser and pushes each committed revision directly; the change store exposes a replayable revision stream so delivery can publish reliably and a reconnecting browser can request only what it missed.
+
+- Operational burden: **weak fit** — connection capacity for 800 concurrent sockets, opening-time connection churn, and reconnect/catch-up edge cases are all ours to build and run.
+- Recurring cost and provider coupling: **strong fit** — no vendor fee, no third party on the critical path; the board's update mechanism is fully within our own infrastructure.
+- Update latency: **strong fit** — push-based, no polling interval to wait out, no repeated reads.
+
+Summary: fastest updates and no provider dependency, bought with connection and reconnect operations we don't currently run.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+A managed pub/sub or realtime-channel vendor brokers publication; delivery wraps the provider's publish API, and browsers use the provider's subscription client instead of a hand-rolled socket protocol.
+
+- Operational burden: **strong fit** — the provider absorbs connection scaling and reconnection internals; delivery's job narrows to reconciling what was published against the change store.
+- Recurring cost and provider coupling: **weak fit** — ongoing per-connection or per-message fees, and the update path now depends on a third party's API stability, pricing, and availability.
+- Update latency: **strong fit** — push-based and low-latency, comparable to self-hosted sockets.
+
+Summary: least operational work and comparably fast updates, at the cost of a recurring bill and a vendor sitting on the board's core update path.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets. Against the major criterion — recurring cost and provider coupling — it ties with polling and beats the hosted service outright; against the moderate criterion — operational burden — it is the weakest of the three. The selection accepts that trade deliberately: the hosted service's chief advantage over self-hosted sockets is operational, not architectural, and that advantage is bought by placing a vendor on the board's core update path, which the recurring-cost-and-coupling criterion weighs more heavily. Polling avoids both the operational burden and the coupling, but loses on latency and read cost at the scale this board needs to run at (800 browsers, 20 edits/second bursts).
+
+This commits the three downstream components to a shape: the change store must expose a replayable revision stream, not just the latest snapshot, so delivery can publish reliably and a reconnecting browser can catch up; delivery owns WebSocket session lifecycle for up to 800 concurrent connections; browser sync must implement reconnect and catch-up logic rather than a stateless re-fetch. It forecloses the lower-operational-burden path the hosted service offered, in exchange for keeping the board's update mechanism independent of a realtime vendor's pricing and availability.
