+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over self-hosted WebSocket connections: the server pushes each committed revision to connected browsers directly, rather than browsers polling for it or a third-party realtime vendor relaying it. Subsecond updates are a byproduct of this choice, not a requirement.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed around this choice. The change store keeps revisions replayable so a reconnecting browser can catch up without missing an edit. Delivery owns WebSocket session lifecycle and reliable publication of committed revisions to connected sessions. Browser sync owns the socket connection and runs reconnect/catch-up logic when a connection drops. Owning connection capacity and reconnect handling ourselves is accepted in exchange for push-speed updates without a recurring realtime-vendor bill or provider coupling.
 
